WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.893
The Delhi edition of the Indian Express
carried a blank editorial on 28 June.

2
00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:12.794
The Financial Express reproduced Rabindranath
Tagore's poem Where the mind is without fear

3
00:00:12.854 --> 00:00:13.966
in large type.

4
00:00:14.106 --> 00:00:18.878
More than 100,000 political opponents,
journalists and dissenters were imprisoned.

5
00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:22.501
The central government imposed strict
censorship on radio,

6
00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:25.218
television and newspapers during this period.

7
00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:31.249
All publications were subjected to substantial
censorship by the Ministry of Information and

8
00:00:31.309 --> 00:00:31.767
Broadcasting.

9
00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:37.502
West Bengal Chief Minister Siddhartha Shankar
Ray proposed that the prime minister impose an

10
00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:38.338
internal emergency.

11
00:00:38.478 --> 00:00:43.210
Indira Gandhi requested President Fakhruddin
Ali Ahmed to proclaim a state of emergency

12
00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.376
later that day.

13
00:00:44.516 --> 00:00:46.485
On 25 June 1975,

14
00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:51.049
Ahmed declared a State of Emergency under
Article 352(1),

15
00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:52.631
citing internal disorder.

16
00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:55.041
When existing laws seemed too slow,

17
00:00:55.101 --> 00:00:57.370
she had the President issue Ordinances,

18
00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.760
bypassing Parliament and ruling by decree.

19
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.358
The Emergency remained in effect until 21
March 1977.

20
00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:06.748
The government introduced press censorship,

21
00:01:06.808 --> 00:01:09.734
postponed elections and banned strikes and
rallies.

22
00:01:09.874 --> 00:01:13.937
Within three hours, the electricity to every
major newspaper was cut.

23
00:01:14.077 --> 00:01:17.676
Police forces were empowered to make
warrantless searches and arrests.

24
00:01:17.816 --> 00:01:25.130
Under MISA, 34,988 people were arrested; under
DISIR, 75,818.

25
00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:30.344
The Government placed thousands of protestors
and strike leaders under preventive detention.

26
00:01:30.704 --> 00:01:32.478
Amnesty International recorded 140,

27
00:01:32.538 --> 00:01:37.204
000 arrests without trial during the twenty
months of the Emergency.

28
00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:38.483
Teenagers faced imprisonment,

29
00:01:38.543 --> 00:01:41.954
with one sixteen-year-old detained for over
eleven months.

30
00:01:42.094 --> 00:01:45.071
The sterilization program reached 8.

31
00:01:45.131 --> 00:01:50.069
3 million procedures in 1976 and 1977, mostly
forced.

32
00:01:50.129 --> 00:01:54.629
Rajan from the Regional Engineering College
suffered torture and died while in police

33
00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:55.089
custody.

34
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.109
The commission found that vagabonds and
beggars were forced into sterilization clinics

35
00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:01.496
by Youth Congress workers and the police.

36
00:02:01.856 --> 00:02:08.105
Mrs Indira Gandhi called elections for March
and released all political prisoners on 23

37
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:09.066
January 1977.

38
00:02:09.206 --> 00:02:14.023
The Janata party won a sweeping victory on 23
March, securing 43.

39
00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:15.788
2% of the popular vote.

40
00:02:15.848 --> 00:02:20.493
The Janata Party manifesto promised to restore
the Constitution to its pre-Emergency

41
00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:20.953
condition.

42
00:02:21.085 --> 00:02:26.077
Morarji Desai became the first non-Congress
prime minister after the party defeated the

43
00:02:26.137 --> 00:02:27.691
Congress (R) in 1977.

44
00:02:27.831 --> 00:02:32.525
The Janata alliance claimed the elections were
India's last chance to choose between

45
00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:33.682
democracy and dictatorship.

46
00:02:34.042 --> 00:02:38.750
Indira Gandhi defended the emergency as
essential for national security despite

47
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.544
offering apologies for abuses.

48
00:02:40.684 --> 00:02:45.308
Critics condemned her authoritarian rule and
cult of personality during the Emergency

49
00:02:45.368 --> 00:02:45.768
period.

50
00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:51.235
The 42nd Amendment faced widespread criticism
for clamping down on civil liberties and human

51
00:02:51.295 --> 00:02:51.695
rights.

52
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.882
The amendment deprived citizens of direct
access to the Supreme Court except for

53
00:02:56.942 --> 00:02:58.124
fundamental rights violations.

54
00:02:58.264 --> 00:02:59.864
In a seven-to-six decision,

55
00:02:59.924 --> 00:03:04.430
the Supreme Court restricted Parliament's
power to alter the Constitution's basic

56
00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:04.905
structure.

57
00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:10.652
The Shah Commission began hearing oral
evidence on 29 September 1977.

58
00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:15.746
It found that the Maintenance of Internal
Security Act and Defence of India Rules were

59
00:03:15.806 --> 00:03:17.143
abused against political opponents.

60
00:03:17.283 --> 00:03:18.718
The final report,

61
00:03:18.778 --> 00:03:21.211
issued on 6 August 1978,

62
00:03:21.271 --> 00:03:22.706
covered prison conditions,

63
00:03:22.766 --> 00:03:23.205
torture,

64
00:03:23.265 --> 00:03:25.259
and family planning atrocities.

65
00:03:25.399 --> 00:03:29.381
Critics later argued that the commission
confused investigation with inquiry.

66
00:03:29.521 --> 00:03:36.314
The commission missed its initial 31 December
1977 deadline and received an extension to 30

67
00:03:36.374 --> 00:03:37.288
June 1978.

68
00:03:37.648 --> 00:03:42.640
Parliament passed an act on 8 May 1979
establishing two special courts.

69
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.028
Indira Gandhi returned to power in January
1980 after a landslide election victory.

70
00:03:48.168 --> 00:03:51.756
The Supreme Court found the special courts
were not legally constituted,

71
00:03:51.816 --> 00:03:53.474
so no trials were held.

72
00:03:53.614 --> 00:03:58.386
Indira Gandhi attempted to recall copies of
the Shah Commission report wherever possible.

73
00:03:58.526 --> 00:04:03.216
The Indian Express carried a blank editorial
on 28 June during the Emergency.
